---
// Blog post: what proper paint prep looks like in Florida and why it matters in Brevard County.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import heroImage from '../../assets/blog/paint-prep-florida-brevard.jpg';

const title = 'What Good Paint Prep Really Looks Like in Florida (Brevard County Guide)';
const description =
  "Florida's climate destroys paint fast if prep is skipped. Here's what proper preparation actually involves in Brevard County — and why it matters more here than almost anywhere else.";

const ogImage = '/images/blog/paint-prep-florida-brevard.jpg';

const floridaChallenges = [
  'Intense UV rays break down paint faster here than in almost any other state.',
  'Constant high humidity slows curing and feeds mildew.',
  'Salt air near the coast eats away at coatings and metal.',
  'Big temperature swings cause surfaces to expand and contract daily.',
  'Frequent heavy rains drive moisture into any small weakness.',
];

const skipConsequences = [
  'Mildew grows back through the new paint',
  'Paint peels in sheets because it was applied over dirt or chalk',
  'Water gets behind walls through bad caulk, causing bubbling and interior damage',
  'Stucco cracks widen and create bigger problems',
  'Cabinets and trim chip at every edge that gets touched',
];

const seeAlsoLinks = [
  { label: 'Homeowner Guide', href: '/homeowner-guide' },
  { label: 'Hiring a Painter in Brevard County', href: '/homeowner-guide/hiring-a-painter-brevard-county' },
  { label: 'Questions to Ask Before You Hire', href: '/homeowner-guide/questions-to-ask-before-you-hire' },
  {
    label: 'Florida Painting License Law Change',
    href: '/blog/florida-painting-contractor-license-changes-brevard',
  },
];

const linkClass = 'font-semibold text-purple transition-colors hover:text-coral';
---

<Layout {title} {description} {ogImage}>
  <main class="page-container py-12 sm:py-16">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-10 text-base leading-relaxed text-charcoal sm:space-y-12 sm:text-lg">
      <header class="space-y-4">
        <figure class="-mx-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl sm:mx-0">
          <Image
            src={heroImage}
            alt="Painter pressure-washing and prepping the stucco exterior of a Florida home in Brevard County"
            widths={[480, 640, 768, 1200]}
            sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px"
            class="aspect-[16/9] w-full object-cover"
            loading="eager"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </figure>
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-purple">Homeowner Guidance</p>
        <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
          What &ldquo;Good Prep&rdquo; Actually Looks Like in Florida — And Why It&rsquo;s Non-Negotiable
        </h1>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          If Walls Could Talk LLC · Brevard County, FL · Homeowner Guidance
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-muted">
          <p>
            If you&rsquo;ve ever had paint start peeling within a couple of years, or watched a brand-new paint job turn
            chalky and faded way too soon, the problem was probably not the paint itself.
          </p>
          <p>It was the prep.</p>
          <p>
            In Florida, prep isn&rsquo;t just a step — it&rsquo;s one of the most important parts of the job.
            Here&rsquo;s why it matters so much in Brevard County, and what good prep actually looks like.
          </p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section class="space-y-4" aria-labelledby="why-florida-heading">
        <h2 id="why-florida-heading" class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold sm:text-3xl">
          Why Florida Makes Prep So Critical
        </h2>
        <p>
          Most of the country has one or two weather challenges. Florida gives paint every challenge at once,
          year-round — and each one compounds the others:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
          {floridaChallenges.map((item) => (
            <li>{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p>
          Because of this, cutting corners on prep doesn&rsquo;t just shorten the paint job — it can cause major damage
          that shows up within 2–3 years instead of 8–10+.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="watercolor-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <section class="space-y-8" aria-labelledby="proper-prep-heading">
        <h2 id="proper-prep-heading" class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold sm:text-3xl">
          What Proper Prep Actually Involves in Brevard
        </h2>
        <p>
          When a painter says they&rsquo;ll &ldquo;prep the house,&rdquo; it can mean almost nothing — or it can mean the
          difference between a job that lasts and one that fails. Here&rsquo;s what good prep should include:
        </p>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold sm:text-2xl">Pressure Washing + Mildew Treatment</h3>
          <p>
            A real pressure wash removes dirt, chalky residue, and loose paint. But washing alone doesn&rsquo;t kill
            mildew. In Florida, you also need a proper mildewcide treatment. If the roots aren&rsquo;t killed, that black
            and green growth will push through your new paint within months.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold sm:text-2xl">Caulking and Sealing</h3>
          <p>
            Every window, door, and penetration needs to be inspected. Cracked, missing, or hardened caulk lets water
            behind the walls — one of the biggest causes of hidden damage in Brevard homes. Fresh, flexible caulk that
            can handle our temperature swings is essential.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold sm:text-2xl">Stucco Crack Repair</h3>
          <p>
            Most homes here have stucco. Small cracks need to be cleaned out and properly filled before painting.
            Painting over active cracks just traps water and makes the problem worse over time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold sm:text-2xl">Priming the Right Way</h3>
          <p>Primer is not optional on most Florida jobs. You need the correct primer for each surface:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
            <li>Bare stucco needs a good bonding or moisture-blocking primer</li>
            <li>Wood needs a penetrating primer to stop tannin bleed</li>
            <li>Metal needs rust-inhibiting primer</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Skipping or using the wrong primer is one of the fastest ways to get peeling paint in our climate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold sm:text-2xl">Interior Prep</h3>
          <p>
            Exterior surfaces get most of the attention in Florida, but interior prep has its own set of requirements —
            and they get skipped just as often. Walls, ceilings, trim, and cabinets each need different treatment: glossy
            surfaces like cabinets and doors must be sanded or chemically deglossed so new paint can stick, trim needs
            cleaning and light sanding, and walls need hole fills and skim work before a single drop of paint goes on.
            Skip any of it and you&rsquo;ll see chipping, peeling, and uneven finish within a year. We&rsquo;ll cover
            interior prep in detail in a separate post.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="watercolor-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <section class="space-y-4" aria-labelledby="skipped-prep-heading">
        <h2 id="skipped-prep-heading" class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold sm:text-3xl">
          What Happens When Prep Gets Skipped
        </h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
          {skipConsequences.map((item) => (
            <li>{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p>
          The sad part? Fixing these issues later always costs more than doing the prep correctly in the first place.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section
        class="space-y-4 rounded-xl border border-purple/20 bg-purple/5 p-6 sm:p-8"
        aria-labelledby="homeowner-tip-heading"
      >
        <h2 id="homeowner-tip-heading" class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold sm:text-3xl">Homeowner Tip</h2>
        <p>
          Take a quick walk around your house and check the caulk around your windows and doors. If it&rsquo;s cracked,
          shrunk, or pulling away, that&rsquo;s water waiting to get inside. Fix it before it becomes a bigger (and more
          expensive) problem.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4" aria-labelledby="bottom-line-heading">
        <h2 id="bottom-line-heading" class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold sm:text-3xl">
          The Bottom Line for Brevard Homes
        </h2>
        <p>
          Proper prep is what separates a paint job that looks good for a few years from one that still looks solid 8–10
          years later in Brevard County. Florida&rsquo;s climate doesn&rsquo;t forgive shortcuts.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you&rsquo;ve seen paint fail early on your own home, there&rsquo;s a very good chance prep (or lack of it)
          was the real reason.
        </p>
        <p>
          Check out our{' '}
          <a href="/homeowner-guide" class={linkClass}>Homeowner Guide</a> for{' '}
          <a href="/homeowner-guide/hiring-a-painter-brevard-county" class={linkClass}>
            tips on hiring a painter in Brevard County
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </section>

      <nav class="space-y-4" aria-labelledby="see-also-heading">
        <h2 id="see-also-heading" class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold sm:text-3xl">See Also</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3">
          {
            seeAlsoLinks.map((item) => (
              <li>
                <a href={item.href} class="font-medium text-purple transition-colors hover:text-coral">
                  {item.label} →
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
          <li class="pt-2">
            <a href="/blog" class="font-medium text-muted transition-colors hover:text-charcoal">
              ← Back to the Blog
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
